nically not classifying  variants in the terms of acmg  but they will give  an  annotation of a disease-causing mutation  or a dm  or if  it's suspected to be a disease-causing  mutation it'll have dm question mark  and so the evidence  is within those publications  and the curators who are reviewing this  evidence  always err on the side of adding  something  rather than leaving it out if there's  any question about its association with  disease so that in future publications  come out this variant is kind of flagged  for  further  review  so that it can be you know revised in  the future as more evidence becomes  available  great  should we be refreshing vcf's upload  from hdmd to ucsc every quarter  yes so every quarter  when the new version of  professional comes out there will be  substantial additions  and new  mutations that are added to the database  so i think it would be a good idea  to upload  the new vcf each quarter just so that  you're reflecting those new additions  to hdmd  and the last question of the session  is uh html integrated into qci if so is  it the full version of hmd or the public  version  yes so qci is qci hydrogen clinical  insights interpret and it's  a software that assists in variant  interpretation  and so with the license of qci interpret  you are able to see the full hdmd entry  for  the version for the variant that you're  looking at  so  what this means is if you wanted to  search for nearby variants  those hdmd entries wouldn't necessarily  show up as part of  qci interpret you would need the  additional license  but for every variant in your vcf that  goes through interpret for which there's  an hdmd  entry you will be able to view that  entry as part of interpret  all right  before we conclude i would like to thank  our speaker dr molina garris and most of  all you our audience for your attention  we hope this was informative and helpful  however if you should have any remaining  questions please do not hesitate to  contact u